Title:
Cronos — Sleep Obfuscation via Waitable Timers (Ekko-inspired)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Cronos is a Windows PoC implementing a sleep-obfuscation technique intended to reduce exposure to memory scanners during idle periods. It leverages waitable timers/APCs to orchestrate encrypting the current process memory with RC4 while “sleeping,” then restoring it afterward. A key element is flipping memory protections (e.g., RW → RX) to make in-memory payload regions less readable during the sleep window. The project is based on ideas from Ekko and references related work like DeathSleep, focusing on evasion tradecraft rather than persistence or exploitation. It’s primarily useful for red team operators, implant developers, and malware researchers evaluating in-memory detection gaps and countermeasures. It’s interesting because it demonstrates a practical, timer-driven control-flow approach to whole-process (or large-region) in-place encryption during sleep.

Technical Focus:
- Sleep obfuscation / in-memory evasion
- Waitable timers and APC-based execution (Windows synchronization primitives)
- RC4 encryption for memory concealment
- Memory protection changes (VirtualProtect; RW/RX transitions)
- C + x86/x64 assembly integration (NASM toolchain)

Use Cases:
- Integrate “encrypted sleep” into a custom loader/implant to reduce memory-scanner visibility
- Prototype and test EDR/memory-forensics detection of transient decrypted windows
- Compare Ekko-style vs timer/APC-driven sleep obfuscation implementations
- Develop defensive detections around suspicious timer/APC patterns and rapid VirtualProtect changes

Keywords:
Cronos, Ekko, sleep obfuscation, waitable timers, APC, Asynchronous Procedure Call, RC4, process memory encryption, VirtualProtect, memory permissions, RWX avoidance, in-memory evasion, Windows internals, NASM, C, assembly, EDR evasion, memory scanner, DeathSleep, red team tooling